From 302f045d1ced58f5e1015ceaa7b12ced2440bb7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 15:12:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(go): install the TypeScript grammar's alternates,
 containers allocated on the open alts

The Go grammar parsed like TypeScript but did not install TypeScript's
alternates. It allocated every map and list in the before-open phase and
re-declared @tabnas/json's `#OB`/`#OS` open alternates without their
@object$/@array$ actions, left the brace-less map alternate and the
top-level leading-comma val alternate without @object$/@array$ and their
static implicit:true config, used a function condition where TypeScript
declares `{ 'prev.u.implist': { $eq: true } }`, kept the list-pair error on
the elem `#KEY #CL` alternate even when list.property/list.pair allow the
pair (TypeScript omits it then), dropped the group tags of the map.child
pair alternate, and named the val close error @val-close-err.

buildGrammar now assembles each rule with the list operations
ts/src/grammar.ts applies to the same @tabnas/json rules (prepend, append,
clear, delete, move), keeping json's alternates by reference with their
actions, and its own alternates are TypeScript's field for field. The
before-open hooks do what TypeScript's do: map bumps dmap; list bumps
dlist and allocates and promotes an implicit list. The Go-only contract
the old BO allocation existed for (a custom BO action can write to the
MapRef/ListRef Meta bag, both_ref_test.go) is kept in state actions:
under info.map/list the BO gives a container that has none yet a
provisional one, and a new after-open hook moves what was written into
the container the alternate allocated.

test/grammar/alternates.json is the TypeScript grammar's installed
alternates per option profile, generated by ts/test/grammar-alts.js from
the engine's rule inspection API and held to the live grammar byte for
byte by ts/test/grammar-alts.test.js. go/grammar_alts_test.go and
rs/tests/grammar_alts_test.rs hold each port's installed alternates to it
(Rust already matched), including which builtin each alternate runs and
its implicit config, with a drift test proving each comparison can fail.

Parse results are unchanged: 978 inputs (every shared fixture input plus
extras) under 10 option profiles give byte-identical results before and
after, BO-phase Meta writes land identically, and every Go, TS and Rust
test and shared fixture passes. Go plugins that entered map or list
through their own alternate and relied on the BO allocation need the
allocation their TypeScript twin already has.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
