-- **Implicit (brace/bracket-less) containers** allocate the container
-  themselves — json's `@object$`/`@array$` only match `#OB`/`#OS`. TS adds
-  `a:'@object$'` with `k.object$={implicit:true}` (the static implicit
-  flag the engine builtins provide) on the implicit map/list open alts; Go
-  allocates the `MapRef`/`ListRef` in the **BO** phase (so a `Meta` bag is
-  available — a Go-only contract) and sets the implicit flag in BC.
+- **Containers are allocated by the open alternates**, in every port:
+  json's `#OB`/`#OS` alts carry `@object$`/`@array$`, and the implicit
+  (brace/bracket-less) ones allocate themselves, because json's only
+  match `#OB`/`#OS`: `a:'@object$'` with `k.object$={implicit:true}` (the
+  static implicit flag the engine builtins provide) on the brace-less map
+  open alt, `@array$` with `k.array$={implicit:true}` on the top-level
+  leading-comma `val` alt, and the `list` before-open hook for an implicit
+  `1,2` / `1 2` list, which promotes the first value into the array it
+  allocates. No before-open hook allocates otherwise, so a plugin that
+  enters `map` or `list` through an open alternate of its own allocates
+  the container itself, as the csv, multisource and toml plugins do in
+  TS. The Go port once allocated every map and list in the
+  **BO** phase instead and re-declared json's `#OB`/`#OS` alts without
+  their actions; Go plugins that leant on that allocation need their TS
+  twin's. Go keeps one Go-only contract on top: under `Info.Map` /
+  `Info.List` a custom BO action can reach the `MapRef`/`ListRef` `Meta`
+  bag, so jsonic's BO gives a container that has none yet a provisional
+  one, and its after-open hook moves what the BO actions wrote into the
+  container the alternate allocated. That is state actions only; the
+  alternates are TypeScript's, which `go/grammar_alts_test.go` and
+  `rs/tests/grammar_alts_test.rs` pin against
+  `test/grammar/alternates.json` (see `test/AGENTS.md`).
 - **`@reset$` on every value-producing relaxed val open alt** (dive,
   top-implicit-map, implicit-null, leading-comma) — mirroring json's
   `#OB`/`#OS`/`#VAL` alts. Omitting it makes a value keep the inherited
@@ -196,10 +212,15 @@ behaviour:
 In TS jsonic owns a phase via the engine plugin-override API
 (`@val-bc/replace`, `@elem-bc/replace`, the `pair`/`val` key alt's `clear`
 alt-mod); `/replace` takes ownership so the builtin is not re-installed on
-later derive. In Go `buildGrammar` mutates the installed rules
-(`ClearActions`/`AddBO`/`AddBC`/`AddAC`) and merges `BuiltinRefs` into its
-local funcref map so code-built alts can resolve `@object$` &c by name
-(the engine auto-merges builtins only for a declarative `GrammarSpec`).
+later derive. In Go `buildGrammar` mutates the installed rules with the
+same list operations the TS grammar applies to them, so json's alternates
+stay where TS keeps them (`PrependOpen` for a TS `open(alts)`, `AddOpen`
+for `append`, `ClearOpen` for `clear`, `ModifyOpen`/`ModifyClose` for
+`delete` and `move`), owns the phases with
+`ClearActions`/`AddBO`/`AddAO`/`AddBC`/`AddAC`, and merges `BuiltinRefs`
+into its local funcref map (`jsonicRefs`) so code-built alts can resolve
+`@object$` &c by name (the engine auto-merges builtins only for a
+declarative `GrammarSpec`).
 Plugins that layer further on jsonic prune rules they don't want via
 `tn.rule(name, null)` (TS) / `j.Rule(name, nil)` (Go) /
 `parser.remove_rule(name)` (Rust).
